  <data key="d3">A 5% decline in the index triggers the 0% floor protection, preventing negative credits; meanwhile, any gain remains limited by the 8.5% cap, illustrating how both bounds shape performance outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d4">enforces limits</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="e56d9b1f-113b-4961-abbd-159d8047aeca">
  <data key="d3">The presence of a 0% floor ensures investors won’t lose money even if the index falls sharply, which complements the 100% participation rate by preserving upside potential without downside risk.</data>
  <data key="d4">enforces</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="f64306b3-60d7-48b6-82c2-6580397642e1">
  <data key="d3">A decline of 5% in the index triggers the protective feature of the 0% floor, guaranteeing that the credited interest does not fall below zero.</data>
  <data key="d4">enforces</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="7898028b-343b-4195-9f4c-a6280a62d640">
  <data key="d3">The 0% floor rule applies specifically during the 1-year crediting period, ensuring investors do not lose money even if the index declines significantly over that term.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="b5a49b5f-16b1-4dba-94df-96a105e6000e">
  <data key="d3">The 0% floor safeguards investors' credited interest when the underlying index falls; thus, regardless of the 50% allocation to the S&amp;P 500 Index, any potential losses from market declines are absorbed due to this built-in protection.</data>
  <data key="d4">protected by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="13737585-4de1-4789-b42c-a05bc511593e">
  <data key="d3">If the NASDAQ-100 Index falls by 5%, the investor’s credited interest cannot go below zero due to the established floor, regardless of the 30% allocation.</data>
  <data key="d4">triggers floor</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="1b43685f-2a0d-488f-9258-b0a8a87ea9f1">
  <data key="d3">A 5% decline in the index triggers a 0% floor on credited interest, protecting investors from losses. This protection complements the 4% minimum cap requirement, ensuring stable and bounded outcomes across varying market scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d4">enforces</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="0f6b3b49-d7b3-4758-a638-6cbce563fda7">
  <data key="d3">The 0% floor safeguards investors from losing money on downturns, making the presence of a minimum 25% participation rate more valuable by protecting potential gains during volatile periods.</data>
  <data key="d4">enhances</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="665dbf69-ad45-49be-8a49-a811282557e1">
  <data key="d3">The 0% floor protects investors from losses due to declining indices, ensuring that even if the index percentage change derived from sentence 1 shows a drop like 5%, no negative interest is applied.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="27176921-2e5c-44aa-bcf0-40ddf6e140f2">
  <data key="d3">The 0% floor acts as a safeguard that modifies the outcome of the interest calculation defined in the formula, preventing negative credits despite adverse index movements.</data>
  <data key="d4">constrains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="6ff1f4bc-a2b4-4e15-b514-415d803bd39b">
  <data key="d3">A floor on interest credited means that regardless of market performance, the minimum return remains at 0%, which aligns with the broader rule stated in fact_sentence_1 about having both a cap and a floor.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="8886f5ce-b28a-43b5-85a1-63104105310a">
  <data key="d3">Even with high participation or favorable index growth, the credit is still bounded above by the cap; however, the presence of a floor ensures that declining indices cannot result in negative returns regardless of the cap's level.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="3fe4c74a-f6e1-4f2e-874b-5a03244e9e44">
  <data key="d3">The presence of a 0% floor ensures that investors do not lose money when the index falls, which directly supports the fairness and safety of the credit calculation system described in the case of rising indices like the S&amp;P 500’s 15% increase.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="a7b1c147-442d-4852-b81d-665875dffa47">
  <data key="d3">The 0% floor prevents any loss on declining indices, directly enabling the guarantee that market downturns won’t lead to negative returns for investors.</data>
  <data key="d4">exemplifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="5af4b2af-c88c-4ee5-ab6b-3214a72b0be8" target="f1ca67d9-884a-4fc4-9ba1-cf7b354e9abd">
  <data key="d3">When the index falls significantly, such as by 5%, the floor mechanism ensures no negative credits, resulting in zero interest being added. This illustrates the worst-case scenario where the policyholder receives no growth despite market decline.</data>
  <data key="d4">exemplifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
