  <data key="d3">The possibility of earning zero interest in the worst-case market scenario arises because the index account includes a 0% floor protection against losses.</data>
  <data key="d4">caused by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f1ca67d9-884a-4fc4-9ba1-cf7b354e9abd" target="b5a49b5f-16b1-4dba-94df-96a105e6000e">
  <data key="d3">The guarantee of zero interest in downturns reflects the protection built into the indexing mechanism, meaning that even with a high allocation like 50% to the S&amp;P 500, investors cannot lose principal—but also may receive no credit during poor market periods.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="f1ca67d9-884a-4fc4-9ba1-cf7b354e9abd" target="13737585-4de1-4789-b42c-a05bc511593e">
  <data key="d3">The 0% floor protects investors against losses in volatile markets, ensuring that even if the NASDAQ-100 index drops, the credited return won’t fall below zero, regardless of its allocation.</data>
  <data key="d4">protects against</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
