<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="50dee1d7-a696-448d-92e6-11dfe14d068f">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual cap exists due to tax qualification requirements, which indirectly supports the idea that premium payments are subject to defined limits—even though they may occur at any time.</data>
  <data key="d4">limited by</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="7d3930c1-6b23-4a04-93b3-d874c9e7b176">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual cap maintains tax-qualified status under IRS guidelines, which indirectly supports the feasibility of setting a lower bound like $100 for flexibility.</data>
  <data key="d4">tax compliance</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="44171d39-27ff-4fcc-bf40-58a3e92080fa">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap exists to maintain tax-qualified status under IRS guidelines, which indirectly supports the feasibility of the $100 minimum as part of a compliant framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">tax qualification</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="b8046f06-ce88-4546-bb7c-16ba4c69bcf2">
  <data key="d3">Tax qualification rules require that annual contributions stay within certain limits, which is why the policy caps payments at $50,000 per year.</data>
  <data key="d4">enforces limit</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="c0561218-4570-4bd7-925f-25cc01fd0c6d">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures compliance with IRS rules for qualified plans, creating a legal boundary within which financial recommendations operate. This regulatory ceiling provides a framework that makes the suggested $6,000 annual contribution feasible and sustainable under tax-qualified guidelines.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual frame</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="2bf05a03-d336-46dd-a382-121bb038f1a6">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures compliance with IRS tax qualifications, providing a legal framework within which the recommended $6,000 contribution operates under stable actuarial expectations.</data>
  <data key="d4">conditions</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="6c580dbf-ec5e-4009-845b-7efb65de3c5a">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures compliance with IRS tax rules, which indirectly supports financial sustainability. This cap helps maintain eligibility for favorable tax treatment, allowing funds like those used for the 3.5% load to remain within qualified plan parameters.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextualizes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="e4d325d0-3102-4ecf-b8a5-e488e84fa28f">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium cap ensures compliance with IRS tax rules, setting a legal upper bound on contributions. This cap influences long-term funding strategy, potentially affecting how frequently or consistently the monthly COI charges are covered from the account balance.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="0d35c1ce-d96d-4562-80f8-0654abe126d2" target="4392497f-15c1-48ee-8067-43a8245bc89e">
  <data key="d3">The $50,000 annual premium limit affects the amount of money invested into the policy, thereby influencing the magnitude of surrender charges if the policy is terminated early.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
