<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="6f98a9e1-688e-4680-b305-6c7ecf379647">
  <data key="d3">A fixed 4% annual interest rate applies only to policy loans that become accessible after one full year of policy activity.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="e35519d3-d4b6-4a91-b9cc-794ecf0231f5">
  <data key="d3">A fixed 4% annual interest rate applies to any policy loan taken, including those reaching the upper limit of 90% of the surrender value.</data>
  <data key="d4">applies to</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="d39b66e7-d38a-450c-a287-bd1bf0a73d22">
  <data key="d3">Loan proceeds are charged a fixed 4% interest rate, yet they continue to accumulate earnings at a 3% fixed account rate, creating a net cost differential.</data>
  <data key="d4">financial trade off</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="16db743e-8a3d-4763-8527-b5d06fd8aafb">
  <data key="d3">The 4% fixed interest rate on policy loans results in a net annual cost of only 1% when combined with the 3% return earned in the fixed account, effectively subsidizing part of the borrowing cost.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="cdffaf84-8d6b-4f83-8142-43784e03289a">
  <data key="d3">A fixed 4% interest rate on policy loans increases the total debt owed over time, leading to greater reductions in the death benefit when claims are processed due to unpaid balances.</data>
  <data key="d4">causes</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="db06e7c6-2f5b-4dca-9e5a-d5dd96d2c405">
  <data key="d3">Policy loans at 4% interest make the 3.0% return on the Fixed Account insufficient to offset borrowing costs, making the Fixed Account less advantageous when used as collateral for loans.</data>
  <data key="d4">financial contrast</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="c4dbdf51-a4cd-4a65-8903-f269d529eefd">
  <data key="d3">Policy loans are charged at 4%, which surpasses the fixed account’s guaranteed 2% return. As such, taking out a loan reduces the overall value of the fixed account unless offset by better-performing investments elsewhere.</data>
  <data key="d4">financial inefficiency</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="9dcea0cf-204b-429a-93cc-c257133b7890">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% loan interest rate complements the information about declining surrender charges, showing how early policyholder actions like taking loans may affect long-term value compared to lapsing or surrendering.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="01ec375d-e72b-4c13-96a2-da5a23238018">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% loan interest rate contributes to the effective cost of borrowing against the policy, which interacts with the 10% surrender charge in Year 1 by influencing whether it's financially prudent to take a loan instead of surrendering the policy early.</data>
  <data key="d4">influences</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="5935cab6-342e-4e3e-9ea8-dbff499af332">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% loan interest rate complements the overall cost structure of the policy, while the 7% surrender charge in Year 4 reflects an additional penalty for early withdrawal, contributing to the total economic burden of policy access.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="00111295-2bb8-4547-912d-c65ce0d8d633">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% interest rate on policy loans complements the understanding of surrender charges like the 6% fee in Year 5 by illustrating different types of financial obligations tied to policy usage.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="29e19c25-f1c1-4c3e-8db6-46bce82c0dca">
  <data key="d3">The 4% fixed loan interest rate complements the understanding of total policy costs, including the 4% surrender charge in Year 7, which reflects another layer of expense when exiting the policy early.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="84b84dec-45b7-4ca8-ae2f-a68466866f24">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% loan interest rate complements the overall cost structure outlined in the surrender charge schedule, where higher early-year penalties reflect the insurer's risk management strategy across multiple policy features.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="e9e8f00a-1414-40a5-99b4-1975b3ddee75">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% loan interest rate contributes to the effective cost of borrowing, which interacts with surrender charges like the 3% in Year 8, influencing total policy expenses over time.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual relevance</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="a3cf559b-392a-434d-8391-67212bc086c3">
  <data key="d3">The 4% fixed loan rate contributes to a net annual cost of 1%, which becomes more relevant in Year 10 when surrender charges drop to 1%, making early surrender less costly relative to borrowing expenses.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="120104c2-987f-4f54-a944-5d235a6dd5df">
  <data key="d3">The fixed loan interest rate affects long-term policy affordability, especially when combined with surrender charges that depend on early premium payments, influencing whether borrowers opt for loans versus withdrawals.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="8e4cefcc-7f56-4cf2-9f4b-3e5dc497847a">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% interest rate on policy loans increases the total debt owed, thereby reducing the cash surrender value when loans remain unpaid. This makes the loan terms financially consequential for the ultimate surrender outcome.</data>
  <data key="d4">depends on</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="b29caae3-f13a-4b43-af52-606c74d4bb86">
  <data key="d3">The 4% fixed loan interest rate remains relevant even in Year 11, where surrender charges drop significantly to 0.5%, reducing barriers to withdrawing funds or repaying loans without substantial penalties.</data>
  <data key="d4">independently relevant</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="9d636a77-9852-432c-b8fb-7593d751a2da">
  <data key="d3">Policy loans incur a fixed 4% interest rate, whereas partial withdrawals allow up to 10% of the account value annually without penalty. While both provide access to funds, withdrawals do not accrue interest like loans, making them more favorable for short-term needs.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="7011576a-0546-4f50-af7e-9f414262d88b">
  <data key="d3">Policy loans at a fixed 4% rate provide borrowing capacity after Year 1, while free partial withdrawals allow access to funds without surrender charges. These two provisions together enhance post-year-one liquidity, offering complementary pathways for accessing policy value.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="e4d325d0-3102-4ecf-b8a5-e488e84fa28f">
  <data key="d3">A fixed 4% loan interest rate increases the cost of borrowing, which could strain the account balance already reduced by recurring COI charges, affecting sustained policy viability.</data>
  <data key="d4">increases financial pressure</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="70667dae-ff8c-4bac-bf04-03807ddb6bd7">
  <data key="d3">The fixed 4% loan interest rate supports the overall risk mitigation strategy implied by the absence of principal loss during market declines, enabling borrowers to leverage policy value securely.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6" target="e9cf97a9-cf24-4d3c-adf8-586c6935d0e1">
  <data key="d3">The 4% fixed interest rate on policy loans is part of the financial framework established by Horizon Wealth Protection Insurance, lending institutional validity to the term.</data>
  <data key="d4">source of context</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="8a1eaedd-d081-4f7b-850d-e908556c1d66" target="84b477e8-9a8c-4a97-b7ac-12df058493f6">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">has fixed interest rate for policy loans</data>
</edge>
