  <data key="d3">A surrender value is paid only if the policy lapses, which occurs at the moment of non-payment per the stated terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="15cc61a2-a432-4cb5-8906-a25d63f7e7f6" target="e27b0a94-4088-4246-ba72-d6793b6cd04d">
  <data key="d3">Failure to pay within the 61-day grace period leads to policy lapse and repayment of the surrender value. Conversely, making a payment during the grace period prevents lapse and maintains coverage.</data>
  <data key="d4">result of</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="15cc61a2-a432-4cb5-8906-a25d63f7e7f6" target="05f6ec15-bbb6-4acf-8ed9-90e66f40d069">
  <data key="d3">The surrender value being returned at lapse provides liquidity upon termination, while the zero downside risk protects against investment loss due to poor index performance. These features together enhance financial safety net provisions.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="15cc61a2-a432-4cb5-8906-a25d63f7e7f6" target="47fe3387-29bc-4869-a3a5-637bc22b6a91">
  <data key="d3">The mention of forfeiting surrender value upon lapse contrasts with the idea of zero risk exposure, suggesting that even without cash value recovery, losses are capped at zero due to structural safeguards.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="15cc61a2-a432-4cb5-8906-a25d63f7e7f6" target="fed316a6-229f-46af-91a9-72511967565a">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 incorrectly suggests the policyholder receives the surrender value after lapse, contrary to fact_sentence_1’s assertion that such value is forfeited under the same condition.</data>
  <data key="d4">contradicts</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
