<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="6e42de55-cde0-435f-b499-10c6b12172e2">
  <data key="d3">Since index participation involves no direct stock ownership, it supports the claim that the policy is not a securities product, aligning with regulatory clarity on the product type and investor protection.</data>
  <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 states that index participation avoids direct stock ownership; this supports fact sentence 2 by reinforcing that it also does not imply owning index funds, further limiting investor control over underlying instruments.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="6d5cb567-6ee5-41f3-8c36-a83d2ff5c7a3">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 explains that participants do not own underlying stocks directly; this context helps explain why dividends from those stocks are excluded from performance calculations, as stated in fact sentence 2.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="7e3f4dce-a967-443e-8c1e-f4c1a24f502c">
  <data key="d3">The absence of direct stock ownership means participants rely on predetermined participation rates, which can vary each year based on plan terms.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="a6b58496-e890-407d-99f3-f8e86cce69dd">
  <data key="d3">The statement that index participation does not involve direct stock ownership provides foundational context for understanding how financial mechanisms like cap rates operate without equity exposure. This helps frame why cap rates might vary independently of market movements.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual relation</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="b080d9ad-b053-459f-8150-83b862b99de1">
  <data key="d3">The statement on non-stock ownership highlights how index participation works without equity exposure, while the guarantee of minimum cap rates ensures predictable limits even during market downturns.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="a416fa11-2b3f-4f38-958d-ff8e13bcbb1a">
  <data key="d3">The absence of stock ownership means investors rely solely on participation mechanics; however, guaranteed minimums ensure these mechanisms still deliver some return regardless of market behavior.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="5ba693f1-db68-45ea-a535-eef1cf763796">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 explains that participants do not own underlying stocks, emphasizing indirect exposure; this context supports why past performance (fact_sentence_2) cannot reliably indicate future gains.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual supports</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="05f6ec15-bbb6-4acf-8ed9-90e66f40d069">
  <data key="d3">The lack of direct stock ownership in index participation ensures that poor index performance cannot reduce the policy's value, thereby guaranteeing protection against losses even when indices decline.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="47fe3387-29bc-4869-a3a5-637bc22b6a91">
  <data key="d3">The clarification that index participation avoids direct stock ownership supports the concept that investors face no loss beyond zero, making zero a safe and desirable result in terms of risk protection.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759" target="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3">
  <data key="d3">Since index participation does not entail actual stock ownership, policyholders cannot claim dividends tied to those securities, especially when the index performs poorly.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="94bf8826-7743-4c63-bbf1-56c4b0008911" target="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759">
  <data key="d2">entity_fact</data>
  <data key="d4">does not involve direct stock ownership</data>
</edge>
