<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="6e42de55-cde0-435f-b499-10c6b12172e2">
  <data key="d3">Since the policy does not involve direct ownership of index funds, it further justifies why the product should not be classified as a securities product under regulatory definitions.</data>
  <data key="d4">justifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="d95da1e8-f071-414b-b0ad-9b56d2f0e759">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 expands on fact sentence 1 by specifying that index participation lacks even indirect exposure through index fund ownership, strengthening the clarification that investors do not hold actual financial securities tied to the index.</data>
  <data key="d4">complements</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="6d5cb567-6ee5-41f3-8c36-a83d2ff5c7a3">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 states that index participation lacks direct ownership of index funds, which contextualizes why dividends from index components are also excluded from performance calculations, as such ownership would typically entitle one to receive them.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="a416fa11-2b3f-4f38-958d-ff8e13bcbb1a">
  <data key="d3">Since index participation does not entail owning index funds directly, safeguards like guaranteed minimum participation rates are essential to protect policyholders' benefits.</data>
  <data key="d4">ensures</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="7e3f4dce-a967-443e-8c1e-f4c1a24f502c">
  <data key="d3">The absence of direct ownership in index funds means participants rely on contractual terms set by the insurer. These terms include the possibility of changing participation rates annually, reflecting adjustments based on market conditions or company policies.</data>
  <data key="d4">modifies</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="5ba693f1-db68-45ea-a535-eef1cf763796">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 states that participants do not own underlying index funds, emphasizing indirect exposure. This contextualizes why historical performance may not reflect future gains, supporting the caution in fact sentence 2.</data>
  <data key="d4">contextual supports</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="47fe3387-29bc-4869-a3a5-637bc22b6a91">
  <data key="d3">The statement about index participation highlights indirect exposure to market gains without fund ownership, supporting the idea that losses cannot exceed zero—a key element behind why "zero" is framed as protective.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="05f6ec15-bbb6-4acf-8ed9-90e66f40d069">
  <data key="d3">The lack of direct ownership in index funds prevents exposure to market downturns, thereby enabling the guarantee that policyholders won't suffer losses even if the index performs poorly.</data>
  <data key="d4">enables</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2ec53e58-1461-418f-af0f-33f4dab5f971" target="4323367b-0222-42cb-99fe-8a2785ec96d3">
  <data key="d3">Since index participation involves no direct ownership of index funds, it naturally means that policyholders do not receive dividends when the underlying index performs poorly.</data>
  <data key="d4">explains</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
